WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.089 --> 00:00:04.630
Film Courage: If Spike Lee didn’t make SHE’S
GOTTA HAVE IT, would you be a filmmaker today?

00:00:04.630 --> 00:00:06.550
Adisa, Writer/Director: Wow?

00:00:06.550 --> 00:00:14.180
For you to ask that question it’s so poignant
because it’s likely that I wouldn’t.

00:00:14.180 --> 00:00:16.570
And it’s very likely that I wouldn’t.

00:00:16.570 --> 00:00:19.240
I knew that I wanted to help people.

00:00:19.240 --> 00:00:28.199
I knew I wanted a career that was going to
service my community possibly around black

00:00:28.199 --> 00:00:35.750
men and what that looked like (rites of passage
programs or whatever).

00:00:35.750 --> 00:00:42.010
But the film thing didn’t become a reality
for me until I saw Spike Lee’s early work.

00:00:42.010 --> 00:00:46.920
I think it has a lot to do with modeling.

00:00:46.920 --> 00:00:53.329
We don’t have a lot of…whether it’s
women or men (black men that is), there is

00:00:53.329 --> 00:00:55.680
something to be said about representation
and modeling.

00:00:55.680 --> 00:00:58.579
And at the time Spike Lee came around there
wasn’t a lot of black filmmakers.

00:00:58.579 --> 00:01:00.020
I mean…that you knew about.

00:01:00.020 --> 00:01:04.269
There was Gordon Parks, he had done some work
(SHAFT).

00:01:04.269 --> 00:01:12.719
And there was Michael Schultz, he had done
COOLEY HIGH.

00:01:12.719 --> 00:01:16.170
I loved the films but I didn’t know particularly
that they were a black man.

00:01:16.170 --> 00:01:21.880
There was something about Spike Lee and his
signature work when he was doing SHE’S GOTTA

00:01:21.880 --> 00:01:29.429
HAVE IT, DO THE RIGHT THING and it was just
so pronounced and so much of a signature around

00:01:29.429 --> 00:01:33.191
black culture and I was like “This is what
I want to do.”

00:01:33.191 --> 00:01:37.670
To answer your question which is a really
good question, I don’t know if I would have

00:01:37.670 --> 00:01:38.670
been a filmmaker?

00:01:38.670 --> 00:01:41.679
Maybe I think I owe my career to Spike Lee.

00:01:41.679 --> 00:01:45.840
I don’t know if I would have been led down
that path, I don’t.

00:01:45.840 --> 00:01:47.249
Yes…that’s really powerful.

00:01:47.249 --> 00:01:51.049
He has had a profound effect on me in terms
of that.

00:01:51.049 --> 00:01:56.929
Film Courage: So you saw the film and then
you decided to attend college at NYU Tisch?

00:01:56.929 --> 00:01:58.090
Adisa: Yes.

00:01:58.090 --> 00:02:03.689
Well I was going back east, for I was going
to University of Massachusetts and then I

00:02:03.689 --> 00:02:07.719
had heard about him making a movie.

00:02:07.719 --> 00:02:13.750
And then I saw the movie in New York on my
way to UMass Amherst I was driving across

00:02:13.750 --> 00:02:15.180
country with a friend.

00:02:15.180 --> 00:02:16.819
I was just amazed.

00:02:16.819 --> 00:02:22.819
And so going into UMass Amherst which was
my junior year I said I’m setting an intention,

00:02:22.819 --> 00:02:24.310
I’m going to be a filmmaker.

00:02:24.310 --> 00:02:26.180
So I was going to do whatever I had to do
to be a filmmaker.

00:02:26.180 --> 00:02:30.660
So I started taking film classes, I started
studying film, I starting make films on film

00:02:30.660 --> 00:02:40.870
like 16 millimeter Bolex (crank cameras) and
then I decided I was going to apply to NYU.

00:02:40.870 --> 00:02:50.409
I made a student film that got a lot of awards
at UMass where I was and I applied to NYU

00:02:50.409 --> 00:02:52.459
because Spike Lee went to NYU.

00:02:52.459 --> 00:02:57.840
So that was sort of my pulse and my geiger,
he was sort of my North Star if you will.

00:02:57.840 --> 00:03:03.110
And I was reading all of the books that he
was reading at that time and I applied to

00:03:03.110 --> 00:03:04.310
NYU and got in.

00:03:04.310 --> 00:03:06.579
Film Courage: Did you ever get to meet him?

00:03:06.579 --> 00:03:08.069
Adisa: I did, I met him first year.

00:03:08.069 --> 00:03:13.780
I met him the first year because he came to…there
was a woman named Lisa Jones who had written

00:03:13.780 --> 00:03:15.239
his books right.

00:03:15.239 --> 00:03:21.430
She was his collaborator on a lot of his film
books and she was at NYU.

00:03:21.430 --> 00:03:24.560
She was a few years ahead of me.

00:03:24.560 --> 00:03:27.000
And I heard when I got there “Spike likes
to meet with the black students,’ right?

00:03:27.000 --> 00:03:29.719
He likes to meet with them first year.

00:03:29.719 --> 00:03:33.689
So he invited us all to 40 Acres and a Mule
Filmworks (his production company).

00:03:33.689 --> 00:03:38.310
And I was like…just you know…starstuck
because he was my idol.

00:03:38.310 --> 00:03:40.799
He is my idol, he’s my hero.

00:03:40.799 --> 00:03:43.310
And I just shook his hand and yes…

00:03:43.310 --> 00:03:48.230
Film Courage: What did he say?

00:03:48.230 --> 00:03:49.400
Adisa: He didn’t say much.

00:03:49.400 --> 00:03:55.779
Spike was a presence more than anything else
because he was really soft spoken back then

00:03:55.779 --> 00:03:59.520
and he let his work speak that was his thing.

00:03:59.520 --> 00:04:02.579
So for a lot of time I was like “Wow, how
is this guy doing this?”

00:04:02.579 --> 00:04:04.879
Because in business you have to be aggressive,
you’ve got to be strong.

00:04:04.879 --> 00:04:06.989
But Spike is very disciplined, he’s very
focused.

00:04:06.989 --> 00:04:10.561
You know you read his books, he would get
up at 6:00 a.m. in the morning, he’d have

00:04:10.561 --> 00:04:14.200
a certain quota he’d have to write on a
screenplay.

00:04:14.200 --> 00:04:19.530
For the first 6 or 7 years he would do a movie
every year it would come out and that was

00:04:19.530 --> 00:04:20.530
his process.

00:04:20.530 --> 00:04:24.240
He would wake up at 6:00 a.m. in the morning,
write and then he’d go about doing his thing.

00:04:24.240 --> 00:04:26.389
But he was a very disciplined individual.

00:04:26.389 --> 00:04:31.760
But yes I was floored, I was floored.

00:04:31.760 --> 00:04:33.479
He was my hero.

